  "query_preview": "..The final day of the official Monsoon Season has \narrived! The main weather story today is gradual clearing as the \nstorm system responsible for the recent heavy rainfall exits the \narea eventually ejecting into the southern and central plains.\n\nStill some residual moisture wrapping around the disturbance, so we \ncan't rule out a few light showers or rumbles of thunder over the \nWhite Mountains and/or Chiricahuas this afternon. However, with best \nforcing associated with the upper low now to our east and with a \ncooler, drier airmass moving in behind the disturbance the threat \nfor heavy rainfall has ended. Thus, anything that develops would be \nlight/showery in nature, associated with residual moisture, and \nwould be forced from the terrain.\n\nHeading into the weekend we'll see a drying trend take shape as a \nridge of high pressure gradually builds across the Desert Southwest \nand into the Great Basin. Temperatures will remain below normal by \n10-15 degrees for early October with an uptick in southeast \nbreeziness over the weekend as a pressure gradient builds from \nhigher pressure sliding down the front range of the Rockies. In \nfact, the latest NBM probability for sustained winds in excess of 25 \nmph across the valleys east of Tucson is generally 30 to 50 percent \nFriday into early next week, with the strongest probabilities Sunday \nand Monday.",
